Dropping this leaves the current residuals unchanged.

- Any code executing in the secure world on a provisioned die at RDP>=1 can derive the PBS and SCP03/admin credentials (DHUK, plus BHK through SAES) and talk to both SEs. It still needs the PIN, bounded by SE-enforced counters, to obtain either half.
- A glitch past the FSBL signature and hash gates runs an arbitrary slot image. This needs a prior flash-write primitive and roughly two faults per boot. #591 covers the evidence and #401 covers the RDP-2 downgrade campaign.
- A vendor-signed malicious update confirmed under PIN is fully trusted (#486).
- A pre-sale implant is caught only by the RDP-0 SWD check and the FSBL fingerprint window.
- A die regressed to RDP0 loses the per-die DHUK (ST constant), so page 126 and the PBS stay protected by that alone.

Even if the HDP variant were built, the attacker would still have:
- one skip on the ACCDIS store or the blank-page branch exposing S_hdp;
- S_hdp remanence on the FSBL stack unless SRAM is scrubbed;
- debug-halt before ACCDIS on a debug-capable regressed die;
- a genuine-image runtime exploit, which holds K and can author the transfer blob;
- phishing the PIN on a fake screen.
